# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY.  Copy this file to `.env.local` and you are done:
#
#     cp .env.local.example .env.local
#
# `.env.local` is gitignored, so your copy can never be committed by accident.
# Next.js loads it ahead of `.env`, so it wins on your machine without you
# having to touch anything else.
#
# Every value below is FAKE and points at your own computer. Nothing here
# reaches a real customer, a real inbox, or a real card. That is the point:
# you should be able to work on this repo for weeks without ever holding a
# production credential, and you can.
#
# The opposite file is `.env.example` — that one is the shape of a REAL
# deployment, and its values live in Vercel, not on anyone's laptop.
#
# Two rules that are not negotiable:
#   1. Never paste a production value into this file. If a value is real,
#      it does not belong in a file whose whole purpose is being handed around.
#   2. Never point DATABASE_URL at production. `prisma migrate dev` and
#      `prisma db push` rewrite the schema of whatever they are aimed at,
#      and there is no undo.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---- Database: your own throwaway Postgres ---------------------------------
# Start one with Docker (deletes cleanly, nothing else on your machine changes):
#
#     docker run -d --name followup-db \
#       -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=localdev -p 5432:5432 postgres:16
#
# Then build the schema:  npx prisma migrate dev
# Check what applied:     npx prisma migrate status
#
# Both URLs point at the same local database on purpose. In production they
# differ (pooled 6543 for the app, direct 5432 for migrations, on different
# roles); locally there is one database and one role, so there is nothing to
# separate.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:localdev@localhost:5432/postgres"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres:localdev@localhost:5432/postgres"


# ---- Login (NextAuth) ------------------------------------------------------
NEXTAUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Signs your local session cookies. Generate your OWN — do not reuse this
# placeholder, and do not ask anyone for the real one:
#
#     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# In production this secret is the single most dangerous value in the file:
# whoever holds it can mint a valid session for any email address, with no
# password. That is why it is per-machine and never shared.
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=""

# Empty = any Google account may sign in. Correct for local; never for deployed.
ALLOWED_EMAILS=""

# Sign-in is Google OAuth only — there is no password login and no dev bypass
# (src/lib/auth.ts). So the two keys below are the ONLY thing standing between
# you and the UI in a browser.
#
# You do NOT need them to write code, run the tests, or open a PR. Leave them
# blank until you actually want to click through the app, then make your own
# free OAuth client (Google Cloud Console -> APIs & Services -> Credentials ->
# Create Credentials -> OAuth client ID -> Web application), publishing status
# "Testing", with BOTH of these Authorized redirect URIs on it:
#
#     http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google
#     http://localhost:3000/api/integrations/gmail/callback
#
# That client is yours. It is not the company's, and it costs nothing.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI="http://localhost:3000/api/integrations/gmail/callback"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything below is deliberately blank.
#
# The app degrades quietly without these — it does not crash, it just does
# less. That is convenient, and it is also a trap: a feature that is off
# because a key is missing looks exactly like a feature that is broken. So
# each one says what actually goes dark, to save you debugging something
# that was never running.
#
# Fill one in only when you are working on that specific thing, and only with
# your own test-mode key.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Scoring and drafting. Blank: leads are captured and stored, but never scored
# and no follow-up is ever drafted. The approval queue stays empty forever.
# Use your own key if you need this; it bills per call.
OPENAI_API_KEY=""

# Encrypts OAuth tokens at rest (src/lib/crypto.ts). Blank: connecting Gmail,
# Outlook, Twilio or Instagram locally will fail to store the token.
# Generate your own:  openssl rand -base64 32
TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""

# Billing. Blank: Settings -> Billing cannot start a checkout. Everything else
# works — `tier: "free"` is real access, not a locked state.
# If you need it, use Stripe TEST mode keys (sk_test_…), never live ones.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=""
STRIPE_PRICE_ID=""
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# Cron authorisation. Blank locally is correct — nothing runs the scheduler on
# your machine. Vercel sends this back as an Authorization header in
# production (see vercel.json). To trigger a cron route by hand locally, set
# any string here and send it as `Authorization: Bearer <that string>`.
CRON_SECRET=""

# Platform admin dashboard (/admin, cross-tenant). Blank: /admin 404s for
# everyone, including you. There is no fallback admin by design. Put your own
# address here if you need to see that screen locally.
PLATFORM_ADMIN_EMAILS=""

# Gmail push notifications (docs/gmail-push-setup.md). Blank: no push. Sync
# still works when triggered by hand — you just do not get the instant path.
GMAIL_PUSH_TOPIC=""
GMAIL_PUSH_SECRET=""

# Live AI voice agent (voice-agent/README.md). Blank: the bridge is not
# reachable. Currently not offered anyway — see CARRIER_CHANNELS_AVAILABLE
# in src/lib/pricing.ts.
VOICE_AGENT_WS_URL=""
VOICE_AGENT_CALLBACK_SECRET=""

# Error monitoring (docs/error-monitoring-setup.md). Blank is a safe no-op —
# nothing is sent anywhere and nothing else changes. Leave these alone locally;
# you want your errors in your terminal, not in the team's Sentry.
SENTRY_DSN=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=""
SENTRY_ORG=""
SENTRY_PROJECT=""
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=""

# Team Slack notifications (src/lib/slack.ts). Blank is a safe no-op, and is
# what you want: with a real webhook here, your local test lead posts into the
# team's actual Slack channel.
SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=""

# One-click Instagram / Facebook connect (docs/meta-oauth-setup.md). Blank:
# Settings falls back to pasting a token by hand — the channel still works,
# it is just not one-click.
INSTAGRAM_APP_ID=""
INSTAGRAM_APP_SECRET=""
FACEBOOK_APP_ID=""
FACEBOOK_APP_SECRET=""

# Outlook / Microsoft 365 capture (docs/outlook-setup.md). Blank: Settings
# shows Outlook as not set up yet. Nothing else breaks.
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=""
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=""
MICROSOFT_REDIRECT_URI="http://localhost:3000/api/integrations/outlook/callback"
